A||||16||Homicidal||Will me amplifier overheat if placed under the seat?||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-12-2000||05:59 PM||||I can't really mount it behind the seat since the cd changer is there.  It's a tight squeeze under the seat, I hope it doesn't damage the amp!||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GTBro||04-12-2000||06:30 PM||||It most likely will overheat under the seat.  Can you put it behind the driver's seat?  Just a suggestion...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||04-12-2000||07:11 PM||||The speaker is going there.  Well, the amp is  150W X 2 channels, and it's not a high quality amp, so do you really think it can get that hot?||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||04-12-2000||07:47 PM||||If it has cooling fins and has enough room for air to circulate through the fins, you should be ok. Maybe you could mount it in the front spare tire compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||04-12-2000||07:51 PM||||Didn't wanna wire so much.  I think I will do  it the ghetto way and just place it in front of the passenger seat with a mat covering it.  I'll move it later when I need to and when I have time, money, and expertise.||||reg||1
Z||000005||overlord||04-12-2000||09:07 PM||||my amp is about 150*2...and is mounted behind the passenger seat.....ive had it cranked for maybe half hour at most and it doesnt get hot to the touch....soo you should be fine..||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||04-12-2000||09:37 PM||||Gotta ask, how big is this CD changer <BR>that you now don't have room behind <BR>the passenger seat? Is it one of <BR>those really oldschool carousel types <BR>or what? I can't picture there not <BR>being room behind the seatback for a <BR>changer and a 2 channel amp. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000007||Standard||04-13-2000||12:03 AM||||Just mount the amp high up, a little below the head rest.  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 04-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Homicidal||04-13-2000||12:28 AM||||I should have been more specific.  No, the changer is a 99 model JVC, smallest 12 cd changer made right now (I think).  BUT, if I mount the amp high, people can easily see it and might wanna take it and then my changer too.  I would rather just cover it with a rug for now and mount it in the front trunk later.<P>It has big fins on the side, does that mean it's a cooling fin?||||reg||1
Z||000009||grizco||04-13-2000||12:44 AM||||I have my amp mounted up high behind the drivers seat but I also have my back window tinted limo black so you can't see the amp.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||04-13-2000||02:28 AM||||Seems like covering it with a rug would be worse than mounting it in a confined space. I know you're in a hurry to get the stereo up and cranking, but (using my best fatherly lecturing tone of voice) you'd be better off in the long run to do it right the first time. Amps are not that hard to wire. The longer the power wire is though, the heavier gauge it should be. Probably a 10 gauge wire will work just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||04-13-2000||07:20 AM||||I still am not getting it I guess, Why not mount the amp and changer both down low? I think the chances of someoneseeing back there are far less then seeing it on the floor of the car. And don't think that after someone yanks your amp off the floor that they won't look behind the seat!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||mbramble||04-13-2000||07:48 AM||||We mounted the 100x4 amp up front in the spare tire of my car.  What my son and I ended up doing was turning the spare tire over so that you have the deep side pointing up, a very large 'bowlfull' of space! I cut out a wood block, about the size of the footprint of the amp, mounted the amp to it and then mounted the block to the spare tire using bolts thru the wood block, thru the lug holes and wingnuts so it can be easlily removed. Then I used large multipin removeable connectors for each side of the amps connections (in- and output sides). To use the spare tire, I remove two connectors, pull the spare tire from the car and remove 3 wingnuts to remove the amp from the spare. Worked out pretty neat. Ran all the wires thru a hole in the frame I found around the steering column that had a plug over it. <BR>The only negative thing I can think of, is that it probably kills any chances of moving the battery to the front. Though I've never seen the details of that mod, I would guess that the deep side of the spare tire turned down provides some of the clearance for the battery?? We could have mounted it with the shallow side up but had very little clearance between the amp and the spare tire hold-down 'strap'. Differing physical dimensions of amps may make this mounting easier, or maybe not practical.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||olympic||04-13-2000||03:16 PM||||   Under no circumstances should you cover an amp with carpet or anything else.  Air must be allowed to circulate over the fins(and body) of the amp.  You dont need much clearance, just nothing touching the amp.<BR>   If you install it under the seat and the amp gets real hot or shuts down you can install a small electric fan to circulate some air.  You can buy them at Radio Shack for a couple of dollars.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Spyder||04-13-2000||03:53 PM||||I have mounted an amp under the passenger side dash where the stock subwoofer would be located, well in that area.||||reg||1
Z||000015||stillofknight||04-15-2000||10:09 PM|||| Did you consider taking out the driver or passenger seat and removing some padding so the amp will have room to breathe? Just an idea.||||reg||1
Z||000016||stimpy||04-16-2000||01:05 AM||||I tried to cut some foam away when I mounted my Bass Shakers...there is a metal wire running through the foam every 4" or so. However, if you cut away the carpeting under the seat you would get bou 1.5" more room. but I'd still pick behind the seat for better access to gain controls and the fuse.<BR>Yanking the seat out to replace a fuse is not a quick deal. ||||reg||1
